WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.250
The Athenian hero of the Greco-Persian war,
the man who devised the tactic that tricked

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:11.140
the Persian navy into an ambush, the man who
set Athens on a course of naval dominance,

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:18.260
Themistocles, was ostracized in c. 471 BCE
from the very city he served so dearly for

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.510
so long.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.300
The reasons behind Themistocles’ ostracism
are far from simple.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.640
Sure, he was becoming too arrogant, and sure,
he was accused of taking bribes, but his ostracism

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.370
was also part of a greater political battle
happening in Athens at the time.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:40.980
In the 470s and 460s, the two most prominent
political debates or movements in Athens could

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.140
be described as the democratic movement and
the conservative movement.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:50.250
Both factions supported the Delian league
and the continuation of the war with Persia,

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.550
but they disagreed about how to handle Sparta
and how to run the Athenian democracy.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:01.280
The conservatives hoped to not necessarily
ally with Sparta but at least not anger them

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.820
or antagonize them.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:08.049
In other words, they wanted to keep the Spartan-Athenian
relations as amicable as possible.

00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:12.970
They also generally wanted a more limited
democracy with most of the political power

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:18.700
resting in the hands of the quote ‘better
classes’ or, in other words, the upper classes.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.320
The democrats, on the other hand, wanted to
hinder the Spartan expansion of power, and

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.750
in general, they wanted to cause as much nuisance
to the Spartans as possible.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.930
The democrats were also in favour of a more
radical democracy.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:37.080
In other words, they wanted to divide the
political power equally among all the Athenian

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.340
citizens regardless of their class.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.750
With that said, Athens didn’t really have
political parties in the way we would understand

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:45.750
them.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:51.240
As Tridimas states ‘in the direct democracy
of Athens, Assembly voters voted on each item

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.450
debated, rather than platforms bundling several
issues … when it is possible to vote on

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:01.890
any single issue, the number of divisions
among citizens is extremely large; every single

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.590
issue may generate a different coalition of
interests.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:10.700
This implies that Assembly voting on each
policy item made impossible the emergence

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.879
of stable, organized groups.’

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:17.760
The people voting for what I called the conservative
movement, or the democratic movement would

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.370
not have seen themselves as part of some democratic
or conservative faction.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:27.450
They were all just people voting according
to their own beliefs, which were often shaped

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.450
by the most prominent politicians of the time
but which, due to how the Athenian government

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:37.599
was structured, did not lead to any organization
of active political parties.

00:02:37.599 --> 00:02:43.300
A source from the late 5th century even mentions
that forming political societies in Athens

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.620
was illegal.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.060
With that said, despite there not being any
political parties, we can, with hindsight,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:55.260
observe political trends and movements happening
in Athens, which often revolved around the

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.430
most prominent politicians of the time.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:02.130
So, when I talk about the conservatives or
the democrats, I am talking about these greater

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.659
political movements, not actual political
parties.

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:11.340
Anyways, for much of the 470s, Athenian politics
seemed to have been dominated by the democratic

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.170
movement, which can be seen through the political
prominence of its largest supporter of the

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.860
time, Themistocles.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:22.390
Themistocles did all he could to hinder any
kind of Spartan plans in Greece.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.210
He tricked them when they protested the rebuilding
of the Athenian walls (see my first video

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.210
in the series).

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:31.160
Through diplomatic cunning, he stopped their
efforts to gain political control over Delphi,

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:36.090
and he specifically pushed for the Athenian
naval build-up to contrast Sparta and its

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.300
land army.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:42.159
According to Plutarch, he was also the champion
of the common people, fighting for their democratic

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:44.580
rights against the conservative movement.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:50.330
However, by the end of the 470s, a new and
very prominent political player emerged in

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:56.100
Athens, Cimon, who initially had the backing
of the late champion of the upper classes,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.100
Aristides.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:02.310
Coming from an old aristocratic family, Cimon
was a staunch conservative who wanted political

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.140
power to be largely held by the upper classes.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:07.290
He also loved the Spartans.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:12.459
He had many friends and allies in Sparta and
was even appointed by the Spartans as their

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:17.600
proxenos, meaning he was chosen by the Spartans
to be their official Athenian diplomat.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.910
Cimon’s initial exploits and victories as
the Delian League’s military leader gave

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:24.080
him much fame in Athens.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.990
After all, he conquered Thrace and Skyros,
found the bones of Theseus, brought a lot

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:33.470
of money to Athens through the slaves and
booty he acquired during his conquests, and

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.289
even funded many new building projects.

00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:41.759
He funded the expansion of the city’s defences
and the rebuilding of the shrine of Theseus,

00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:44.290
the Athenian Academy, and the Acropolis.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:49.449
He also funded an overall refurbishment of
the city, fixing old roads, adding new trees

00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:53.480
and gardens and draining the swamps between
Athens and Piraeus.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:59.110
All these military exploits and building projects
made Cimon very popular in Athens, which in

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:04.050
turn made the conservative movement, which
he championed, also popular.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:10.120
Hence, Themistocles’ ostracism in c. 471
can be seen as a pivotal moment in Athenian

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.750
politics when the conservative movement, thanks
largely to Cimon, started to dominate over

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.600
the democratic movement.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.600
The Spartans used the fact that the conservative
pro-Spartan movement was at its height in

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.520
Athens to their advantage.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.120
Ever since Themistocles tricked them during
the whole Athenian wall-building project,

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.710
they hated him with a passion and wanted to
see him destroyed.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:39.460
So, when he was ostracized, Sparta seized
the opportunity and came forth with evidence

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.069
that implicated Themistocles in Pausanias’
plot.

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:48.000
Pausanias was the Spartan general accused
of working with the Persian king to overthrow

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.330
Greece . Athens took the Spartan accusations
seriously and either tried and declared Themistocles

00:05:54.330 --> 00:06:00.360
guilty in absentia or sent soldiers to retrieve
Themistocles from exile in order to be tried

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.460
in Athens.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.020
The sources are unclear.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:07.850
All we know is that Athens did send soldiers,
along with some Spartan volunteers, to take

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:09.840
Themistocles into custody.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.230
Themistocles, knowing he was either already
declared guilty of conspiring with the Persian

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:20.081
King or that he would be declared guilty in
the now conservative-leaning Athens, tried

00:06:20.081 --> 00:06:24.960
fleeing to various places around Greece, but
every time he would arrive somewhere, he would

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.940
be soon forced to leave due to the relentlessness
of the soldiers hunting him.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:35.050
With no other choice, Themistocles decided
to flee Greece entirely to the very kingdom

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.460
he was accused of conspiring with, Persia.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.819
Whether Themistocles was actually guilty,
as well as Pausanias, is honestly impossible

00:06:43.819 --> 00:06:44.819
to know.

00:06:44.819 --> 00:06:48.979
After all, it all happened 2500 years ago.

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:53.729
When Themistocles arrived in Persia, he met
with the king and was said to have impressed

00:06:53.729 --> 00:06:58.650
the king so much that he was given a small
governorship in Asia Minor, bordering the

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.919
Ionian cities of the Delian League.

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:06.530
Also, while all of this was happening, Themistocles’
wife and kids, along with some of his possessions,

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:09.889
were smuggled to him out of Athens by some
of his friends.

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:14.930
Themistocles would eventually die in Asia
minor while serving the Persian king as his

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:20.400
governor, and so ended the life of one of
the most well-known military tricksters of

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.229
the Greco-Persian War.

00:07:22.229 --> 00:07:27.840
Meanwhile, in the Delian League, after the
brief respite in the war with Persia due to

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:35.300
the Naxian rebellion, Cimon, in c. 467, planned
a Delian League expedition to quote ‘liberate’

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.970
more Greek cities and conquer Persian ones
located in Lycia and Pamphylia.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:45.449
In preparation for this expedition, Cimon
widened the Athenian triremes in order for

00:07:45.449 --> 00:07:51.190
them to be able to hold more hoplites on their
decks, so when they would board enemy ships,

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.490
they would always have a numbers advantage.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:59.680
With about 200 of these widened triremes,
Cimon set sail for Asia Minor.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.520
As the Delian League forces were conquering
the coastline of today’s southwestern Turkey,

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:10.410
Cimon found out that much of the Persian navy
, about 350 triremes strong, was anchored

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:15.930
at the mouth of a nearby river called Eurymedon,
hiding from the Athenians and waiting for

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.740
naval reinforcements to come from Cyprus.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:23.879
Cimon, hoping to defeat the Persian navy before
it could be reinforced, quickly attacked the

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:25.300
anchored navy.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:29.560
The Persians didn’t want to fight, hoping
to wait for the reinforcements, but Cimons

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:35.570
quick attack forced their hand, and so a battle
ensued in which the widen triremes of Cimon

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:40.519
proved extremely successful, and the Persian
navy was forced to retreat, running their

00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:45.220
ships aground and fleeing to meet up with
the Persian army stationed nearby.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:51.360
Cimon, eager to push his advantage, also disembarked
his troops and proceeded to engage with the

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.360
Persians.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.880
According to Diodorus, the Persian army did
not expect to encounter a sizable enemy, for

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.690
they thought the Greeks only had a naval force
in the area and were unaware of the sizeable

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.700
number of hoplites on the widened triremes.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:09.560
Therefore, when Cimon attacked with all his
disembarked hoplites, the Persians were caught

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:13.480
off guard by their sheer numbers and promptly
lost the battle.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.279
Many of the Persian sailors and soldiers were
enslaved, their camp was looted, and their

00:09:18.279 --> 00:09:20.180
ships destroyed.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:24.970
According to Plutarch, this victory was still
not enough for Cimon, who forced a portion

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:30.700
of his navy back out to sea to confront the
incoming Persian naval reinforcements.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:35.450
Caught off guard and unaware of what happened
to the main Persian navy, the remaining Persian

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:39.180
navy, in a confused panic, was easily defeated.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:45.089
With this one land and sea battle, Cimon managed
to destroy most of the Persian navy, and Persia

00:09:45.089 --> 00:09:50.220
ceased to be a serious naval threat to Greece
for the next 17 years.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:55.430
Cimon returned to Athens, even more of a hero
than he already was, bringing with him more

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:59.510
slaves, more booty, and yet more expansion
of the Delian League.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:05.019
Whether the new cities in Lycia and Pamphylia
wanted to actually join the league or were

00:10:05.019 --> 00:10:10.520
forcefully coerced by Athens to join is hard
to say and has been a matter of debate by

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:11.520
historians.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.269
Nevertheless, this did not matter to the Athenians,
who certainly must have seen the expansion

00:10:16.269 --> 00:10:20.920
of a league of which they were a hegemon as
a positive.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.600
Cimon’s ever-growing popularity expanded
the power of the conservative movement in

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.399
Athens even more, which started to threaten
the Athenian democratic system.

00:10:30.399 --> 00:10:34.500
This is because a lot of the conservatives
tended to favour a limited democracy.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.649
The people who voted for the conservative
agenda wanted most of the political power

00:10:38.649 --> 00:10:44.339
to be held by the upper classes or, as Aristotle
called them, the ‘better classes’ rather

00:10:44.339 --> 00:10:48.320
than sharing political power with the inexperienced
rabble.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:53.050
This is not to say that all the conservative
voting people were only from the upper classes.

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:57.389
There certainly were poorer people that argued
for the conservative cause.

00:10:57.389 --> 00:11:01.709
After all, most Athenians were not part of
the upper class, so the fact that the conservative

00:11:01.709 --> 00:11:07.190
movement was so prominent must also imply
some sort of support from the lower classes

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:08.190
.

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:12.320
Now, in order to understand what Cimon and
the conservatives were doing to undermine

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:18.139
the Athenian democracy, we must first understand
the current political system of Athens, which

00:11:18.139 --> 00:11:23.310
began in the late 6th century, and to help
me explain that system here’s a small but

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:26.560
very knowledgeable historytuber, Nothing New.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.329
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Thanks Matus (pronounced Matoos), the Athenian

00:11:28.329 --> 00:11:33.300
system of government in c. 465 was built on
the reforms of Cleisthenes, which were enacted

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.710
around 508-507.

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:40.510
Before these reforms, Athens was divided into
three main factions, the shore, the hill,

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:43.709
and the plain, which were headed by various
aristocrats.

00:11:43.709 --> 00:11:48.089
This meant that a politician like Pisistratus
could use his popularity to seize power and

00:11:48.089 --> 00:11:52.140
establish a single-rule tyranny, which often
seemed like a preferable option compared to

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.730
a city dominated by fierce rivalries between
factions.

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:59.220
These were the issues which Cleisthenes would
have to solve in order to secure the democracy

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.410
that Athens would become famous for: he needed
to break up the traditional tribes and the

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:07.700
strong influence of the old noble families,
and he needed to find a way to stop particular

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.770
politicians from gaining too much power through
popularity.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:14.949
Cleisthenes achieved this by reforming the
political system of Athens, which had existed

00:12:14.949 --> 00:12:17.150
since the earlier reforms of Solon.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:22.529
First, Cleisthenes abolished the four traditional
Ionian tribes that divided Athens and reorganized

00:12:22.529 --> 00:12:24.430
the city into ten new tribes.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:29.649
He did this by dividing the three main geographical
areas into multiple smaller subdivision s

00:12:29.649 --> 00:12:34.260
and then took the new subdivisions from each
geographical area and combined them to form

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.279
10 completely new tribes.

00:12:36.279 --> 00:12:41.060
This effectively broke up the old Athenian
factions and the power of the noble families.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:46.251
Conscription, elections, jury appointments,
and more were all done through these new 10

00:12:46.251 --> 00:12:47.251
tribes of Athens.

00:12:47.251 --> 00:12:50.899
Second, Cleisthenes gave more power to the
Athenian Assembly.

00:12:50.899 --> 00:12:55.209
The Assembly allowed every Athenian citizen
to speak their mind and vote on all matters

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:59.269
concerning the city-state, its laws, and elected
government officials.

00:12:59.269 --> 00:13:03.460
While the Assembly included all citizens,
we should keep in mind that this excluded

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:07.610
women, children, (knowns as metics) foreign
residents, and slaves, who were denied the

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.250
rights of citizenship.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:13.100
Under Cleisthenes, the Assembly essentially
became the most important branch of government

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:14.100
in Athens.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:18.930
Third, Cleisthenes also gave more power to
the council of 500, known as the Boule, which

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:23.040
guided and prepared the agenda for the Assembly
and functioned as the executive branch of

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:24.040
the government.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:27.730
The reorganization of the tribes required
Cleisthenes to expand the Boule from four

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:32.550
hundred to five hundred members, with fifty
members chosen annually by random selection

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.889
from a group of volunteers coming from each
of the ten tribes.

00:13:35.889 --> 00:13:39.790
Fourth, Cleisthenes took away much of the
power of the nine archons, which were the

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:41.870
chief magistrates of the city.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:46.230
Before Cleisthenes’ reforms, the Archons
were the main executive branch of the government,

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:50.449
but since Cleisthenes gave much of this power
to the Boule, the archons became symbolic

00:13:50.449 --> 00:13:55.310
figureheads overseeing religious, military,
and political celebrations (and events).

00:13:55.310 --> 00:14:00.920
By c. 465, the archons, just like the members
of the Boule, were elected by lot from a group

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:04.339
of 500 volunteers, again 50 from each tribe.

00:14:04.339 --> 00:14:09.589
Fifth, Cleisthenes reorganized the military,
with each tribe electing an infantry commander,

00:14:09.589 --> 00:14:13.140
a cavalry commander, and most importantly,
a general.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:18.320
The board of the ten generals , known as strategoi,
oversaw all matters relating to the Athenian

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:21.660
military and later also the Delian League
military.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:27.100
By c. 465, the strategoi were the only high-ranking
government office in Athens that was elected

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:30.680
by popular vote and could also serve unlimited
consecutive terms .

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:35.480
Sixth, even though the Assembly served as
the final appeals court for most criminal

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:40.120
cases, Cleisthenes established a court system
for minor cases where jurors were selected

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.540
by lot from an annual roll of 6000 volunteers.

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:47.550
The Areopagus was the only branch of the Athenian
government that seems to have been untouched

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.199
by Cleisthenes’ reforms.

00:14:49.199 --> 00:14:53.910
The Areopagus was a council of elders composed
of former archons, and its main function was

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:58.530
to scrutinize elected and government officials
and to judge serious crimes such as murders

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:01.620
.
Lastly, we should touch upon the use of sortition

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:06.370
in Athens, which was the process of choosing
government officials by random selection from

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:10.089
a group of volunteers, also known as election
by lot.

00:15:10.089 --> 00:15:13.949
We don’t know when the first instance of
sortition occurred in Athens, but it most

00:15:13.949 --> 00:15:18.430
likely emerged at some point in the 6th century,
with the largest expansion of its use taking

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:20.690
place during Cleisthenes’ reforms.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:25.010
Initially, historians thought that the use
of sortition had a religious aspect in the

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:28.779
sense that the random result of the lot revealed
the will of the gods.

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:33.700
However, in recent years a closer examination
of the primary sources showed no connection

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:39.149
between election by lot and religion, meaning
that Athenians chose sortition due to perceived

00:15:39.149 --> 00:15:42.570
practical benefits rather than religious reasons.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:47.560
The actual practicality of election by lot
is a debate for another time but, as Hansen

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:51.990
states, ‘in the selection of magistrates
the Athenians preferred the lot to election

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:57.200
because it safeguarded the powers of the people
and obviated factionalism and corruption’,

00:15:57.200 --> 00:16:00.830
which was yet another reason why Athens didn’t
have political parties.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:04.360
This is the general picture of the Athenian
government in the fifth century that scholars

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:08.170
have been able to piece together, but we should
keep in mind how much we don’t know about

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:11.040
the exact functions of the different bodies
of government.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:15.231
This overview was pretty brief in describing
the functions of the various branches of the

00:16:15.231 --> 00:16:19.060
Athenian government simply because we just
don’t know that much about them.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:24.329
Our understanding of Classical Athenian democracy
is incomplete because it relies entirely on

00:16:24.329 --> 00:16:29.360
fragmentary inscriptions and testimonies of
later writers such as Aristotle and Herodotus.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:33.660
It is also due to the nature of these sources
that while certain reforms are attributed

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:37.130
to legendary figures like Cleisthenes and
Solon, it is actually hard to say whether

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:41.410
Cleisthenes was responsible for instituting
everything attributed to him.

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:45.680
None the less, if you want to learn more about
Cleisthenes and other famous Athenian lawmakers,

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:49.779
head over to my channel and check out the
videos I made all about their lives and reforms.

00:16:49.779 --> 00:16:50.779
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

00:16:50.779 --> 00:16:51.779
Thank you Nothing New.

00:16:51.779 --> 00:16:53.490
Definitely go check him out!

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:59.230
As you can see, the exact functions of all
the Athenian branches of government in c.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.880
465 are not entirely known to us.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:06.949
However, according to Aristotle and Plutarch,
during this time, Cimon and his conservative

00:17:06.949 --> 00:17:12.799
allies were using the power of specifically
the Areopagus to control Athenian politics.

00:17:12.799 --> 00:17:15.030
The way they did this was simple.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:18.240
The Areopagus was composed of only former
archons.

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:22.160
In other words, people that already held a
political position.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:28.480
As previously mentioned, archons were elected
by lot from a group of volunteers, so in theory,

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:33.210
any Athenian citizen could have become an
archon, but there were several caches.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:37.400
First, none of the positions of the Athenian
government were paid.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:41.410
Meaning only people who could financially
support themselves while doing their political

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:47.270
duties could realistically volunteer to be
elected by lot into government positions,

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:52.160
which meant that much of the government ended
up being filled by the upper classes, including

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:53.490
the Areopagus.

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:59.679
Second, one of Areopagus’ powers was to
quote ‘scrutinize’ incoming government

00:17:59.679 --> 00:18:02.620
officials to ensure they were fit for the
job.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:06.480
For example, making sure they don’t have
any criminal records or that they don’t

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:10.530
have any conflict of interest with their appointed
office, stuff like that.

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:15.820
Areopagus also had the power to prosecute
government officials once they were in office

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.110
or even after their term ended.

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:22.970
This power of scrutiny over the government
officials when they were elected, when they

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:27.919
were in office, and even when their office
ended meant that the Areopagus, even though

00:18:27.919 --> 00:18:33.030
it couldn’t control the elections, could
control who would eventually become a government

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:38.280
official and through threats of prosecution
could even control their decisions while in

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:39.280
office.

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:45.049
Essentially, the Areopagus, which had an upper-class
majority, could control the membership of

00:18:45.049 --> 00:18:50.900
all the elected branches of government making
sure the ‘better classes’ stayed in control.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:56.380
This political power of the Areopagus became
increasingly utilized by Cimon and his conservative

00:18:56.380 --> 00:19:00.260
allies in the late 470s and early 460s.

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:04.970
According to Aristotle, during this time,
the Aeropagus had the ‘superintendence of

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:07.040
affairs’ of Athens.

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:12.000
With that said, no matter how much political
control Cimon and the conservatives had through

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:16.190
the Areopagus, they still could not control
the most important branch of the Athenian

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:18.010
government, the Assembly.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:23.760
The Assembly was where every Athenian citizen
could voice their opinion regardless of rank

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:28.440
or status, and it was here that the democratic
movement, which has been in decline since

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:33.720
the ostracism of Themistocles, found two new
quote ‘leaders of the people’.

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:36.010
Ephialtes and Pericles.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:41.789
Both men came onto the political stage in
the 460s as Athenian strategoi, and they increasingly

00:19:41.789 --> 00:19:46.990
started to be vocal in the Assembly against
Cimon, the conservatives, and the overreach

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:48.390
of power by the Areopagus.

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:54.480
Ephialtes was initially the more successful
democratic politician out of the two because,

00:19:54.480 --> 00:20:00.010
unlike Pericles, who had an upper-class background,
Ephialtes, according to Aelian, came from

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:04.630
‘extreme poverty’ and so could connect
with the common people better than Pericles

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:05.630
could.

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:10.780
Since Cimon was the head of the Delian League
military, he was often away from Athens on

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:16.780
military expeditions, and it was these intermittent
absences of Cimon that Pericles and Ephialtes

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:17.780
exploited.

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:22.700
For example, when Cimon, after the battle
of Eurymedon, went to quote ‘liberate’

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:28.140
the cities of the Sea of Marmara , Pericles
and Ephialtes used Cimon’s absence to attack

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:33.390
him and the conservatives with everything
they could think of; from basic slander like

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:39.530
stating that Cimon was sleeping with his sister,
to more serious crimes like charges of bribery,

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:42.750
or accusations of undermining the Athenian
democracy.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:48.110
However, despite these efforts by the new
leaders of the democratic movement, conservatives

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:53.570
still retained control of the Athenian government
mostly due to the still tremendous popularity

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:54.750
of Cimon.

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:58.580
But popularity in the public’s eye doesn’t
last forever.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:04.781
Especially when Ares and Poseidon were eager
to test just how popular Cimon was, for a

00:21:04.781 --> 00:21:11.260
new war and a natural disaster were just around
the corner.

